# Code Quality Validation Rule

## Purpose & Scope

Defines comprehensive code quality validation standards for multiple programming languages. This rule ensures code follows best practices, is maintainable, and meets industry standards for production-quality software development.

**Applies to**: All source code files before code review, merge, or deployment

**Does not apply to**: Configuration files, documentation, test data, or build artifacts

## Inputs (Contract)

- Source code files in supported programming languages
- Linter tools available for each language
- Test environment for execution validation
- Code review checklist
- Performance profiling tools (optional)

## Outputs (Contract)

- Code quality score and metrics
- Linter error and warning counts
- Code complexity metrics
- Security vulnerability assessment
- Performance impact analysis
- Validation report with specific issues and recommendations

## Core Principles

**NEVER merge code that fails quality standards.**

**Code quality is not subjective - it's measurable and enforceable.**

**Prevention is better than cure - catch issues early in development.**

## Mandatory Quality Gates

### Gate 1: Syntax and Basic Standards (REQUIRED)

#### Python (flake8 + mypy + black)
- [ ] Zero flake8 errors (E, F, W codes)
- [ ] Zero mypy type errors
- [ ] Black formatting compliance (line length 88)
- [ ] Import organization (isort compliance)
- [ ] No bare except clauses
- [ ] No wildcard imports

#### JavaScript/TypeScript (ESLint + Prettier)
- [ ] Zero ESLint errors
- [ ] Prettier formatting compliance
- [ ] No console.log statements in production code
- [ ] Proper type annotations (TypeScript)
- [ ] No unused variables or imports

#### Java (Checkstyle + SpotBugs)
- [ ] Zero Checkstyle violations
- [ ] No SpotBugs security warnings
- [ ] Proper Javadoc documentation
- [ ] No raw types
- [ ] Proper exception handling

#### C/C++ (clang-tidy + cppcheck)
- [ ] Zero clang-tidy warnings
- [ ] No cppcheck security issues
- [ ] No memory leaks (Valgrind clean)
- [ ] Proper resource management
- [ ] No buffer overflows

### Gate 2: Code Complexity and Maintainability (REQUIRED)

#### Cyclomatic Complexity
- [ ] Functions: Max 10 complexity (Python/JavaScript), 15 (Java/C++)
- [ ] Classes: Max 20 complexity (Java/C++), 15 (Python/JavaScript)
- [ ] Modules: Max 50 complexity overall

#### Code Duplication
- [ ] Less than 3% duplicated code (PyCodeStyle/Copy-Paste Detector)
- [ ] No identical functions across modules
- [ ] Common patterns extracted to utilities

#### Documentation Quality
- [ ] All public functions/classes have docstrings/comments
- [ ] Complex algorithms explained
- [ ] API documentation complete
- [ ] Usage examples provided

### Gate 3: Security and Safety (REQUIRED)

#### Input Validation
- [ ] All user inputs validated and sanitized
- [ ] SQL injection prevention (parameterized queries)
- [ ] XSS prevention (proper escaping)
- [ ] Path traversal prevention

#### Authentication and Authorization
- [ ] Proper authentication mechanisms
- [ ] Role-based access control
- [ ] Session management secure
- [ ] Password handling secure

#### Data Protection
- [ ] No hardcoded credentials
- [ ] Proper encryption for sensitive data
- [ ] Secure random number generation
- [ ] Proper error handling without information leakage

### Gate 4: Performance and Efficiency (HIGHLY RECOMMENDED)

#### Algorithmic Efficiency
- [ ] No O(n²) algorithms where O(n) exists
- [ ] Proper data structures used
- [ ] Caching implemented where appropriate
- [ ] Database queries optimized

#### Resource Usage
- [ ] Memory usage within acceptable limits
- [ ] No memory leaks detected
- [ ] Proper connection pooling
- [ ] Efficient I/O operations

#### Scalability
- [ ] Code handles concurrent access
- [ ] Proper locking mechanisms
- [ ] Thread-safe implementations
- [ ] Load testing performed

## Language-Specific Validation Rules

### Python Specific Rules

#### PEP 8 Compliance
- [ ] Line length ≤80 characters (configurable)
- [ ] Proper indentation (4 spaces)
- [ ] Blank lines between functions/classes
- [ ] Import organization (standard library, third-party, local)

#### Type Safety
- [ ] All function signatures have type hints
- [ ] Return types specified
- [ ] Complex data structures typed
- [ ] No Any types (except for specific cases)

#### Error Handling
- [ ] Specific exceptions caught, not bare except
- [ ] Custom exceptions defined for domain errors
- [ ] Error messages informative but not revealing sensitive data
- [ ] Resource cleanup in finally blocks

### JavaScript/TypeScript Specific Rules

#### TypeScript Strict Mode
- [ ] Strict mode enabled
- [ ] No implicit any types
- [ ] Proper interface definitions
- [ ] Type guards implemented

#### React/Node.js Specific
- [ ] No direct DOM manipulation (React)
- [ ] Proper useEffect cleanup
- [ ] No memory leaks in event listeners
- [ ] Proper state management

### Java Specific Rules

#### Object-Oriented Design
- [ ] Single responsibility principle followed
- [ ] Proper encapsulation
- [ ] Inheritance used appropriately
- [ ] Interfaces preferred over concrete classes

#### Memory Management
- [ ] No memory leaks
- [ ] Proper garbage collection
- [ ] Resource cleanup in finally blocks
- [ ] Proper use of try-with-resources

### C/C++ Specific Rules

#### Memory Safety
- [ ] No buffer overflows
- [ ] Proper null pointer checks
- [ ] No use-after-free
- [ ] Proper memory allocation/deallocation

#### Pointer Safety
- [ ] No dangling pointers
- [ ] Proper ownership semantics
- [ ] Smart pointers used appropriately
- [ ] No pointer arithmetic where not needed

